course: biology
lesson: 234
title: Animal minds: the octopus, escape artist and puzzle solver
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: An octopus is a soft relative of snails and clams, yet it is one of the cleverest animals in the sea.
explain: Put a crab in a screw-top jar and an octopus will feel it, test it and then unscrew the lid, and some have learned to open a jar from the inside.
explain: Its brain is spread out: one main brain in the head, and big bundles of nerve cells in each of its eight arms, which taste and feel everything they touch.
explain: It has no bones, changes colour in a flash and squeezes through tiny gaps, so aquarium keepers call it the escape artist of the sea.
explain: Strangest of all, a baby octopus never meets its parents, so everything clever it does, it works out on its own.
fact: Octopuses can learn to open screw-top jars, even from the inside, and their arms can taste and feel what they touch.
fact: An octopus has one main brain, not nine, but about two thirds of its nerve cells are in its arms, which do much of their own thinking.
fact: An octopus has no bones; its only hard part is its beak, so it can squeeze through any gap bigger than the beak.
fact: In 2016 an octopus called Inky was reported to have escaped from a New Zealand aquarium through a gap and down a drainpipe to the sea.
fact: Octopuses change colour in a flash with millions of tiny colour sacs in their skin, to hide, show mood or startle enemies.
fact: Octopuses are thought to be colour-blind, yet they match their background so well that scientists still argue about how.
fact: The veined octopus of Indonesia carries coconut shell halves and closes itself inside them when danger comes, a kind of tool use.
fact: Octopuses can recognise people, and one in a lab squirted water at a keeper it disliked, and one in a German aquarium squirted water at a bright lamp that bothered it until the lamp short-circuited.
fact: Many octopuses live only one or two years; the mother guards her eggs until they hatch and then dies, so babies learn everything alone.
fact: An octopus has three hearts and blue blood, because its blood uses copper instead of iron to carry oxygen; its cousin the squid has three hearts too.
fact: In a test like the marshmallow test, cuttlefish waited for a shrimp they liked rather than take a less-liked crab at once.
fact: Gloomy octopuses in Australia were filmed throwing silt and shells with a jet of water, sometimes at each other.
fact: The mimic octopus, discovered in 1998 off Indonesia, copies the shape and movement of dangerous animals like sea snakes and flatfish.
exercise: What can an octopus do with a screw-top jar? | Unscrew it to get the food inside, even from the inside | Only look at it until a keeper comes to open it | Break it with its tail | Nothing, it has no grip strong enough to turn a lid at all
exercise: What can an octopus's arms do besides grab? | Taste and feel what they touch | See colours and shapes | Hear sounds far off in the sea | Breathe air
exercise: How many brains does an octopus really have? | One main brain, plus big nerve bundles in each arm | Nine full brains | Eight brains, one in each arm and none in the head at all | No brain at all
exercise: Where are most of an octopus's nerve cells? | In its arms | In its big head | In its hearts | In its ink sac
exercise: Why can an octopus squeeze through a tiny hole? | It has no bones, only a hard beak | It can shrink its beak to any size it likes | It melts like jelly | It breaks the hole wider
exercise: What was Inky the octopus reported to do in 2016? | Escape from an aquarium down a drainpipe to the sea | Paint a picture | Open the aquarium shop door every morning for the visitors | Learn to talk
exercise: How does an octopus change colour so fast? | With millions of tiny colour sacs controlled by muscles and nerves | By eating coloured food | By changing the colour of its blood from blue to red and back again | By swapping its skin
exercise: Why does an octopus change colour? | To hide, show its mood or startle enemies | Only because the water around it is too cold | Because it is getting older every single day | To keep its soft skin clean in the dirty sea
exercise: What does the veined octopus do with coconut shells? | Carries them and hides inside when danger comes | Eats them | Uses them to crack open crabs and mussels | Throws them at boats that sail too close to its home
exercise: How did an octopus in a lab treat one keeper? | It squirted water at that keeper only | It hugged that keeper only, every morning | It ignored all of the keepers just the same | It followed that keeper home after work
exercise: What happens to an octopus mother after her eggs hatch? | She usually dies | She teaches her babies for years | She lays more eggs every week | She becomes a male
exercise: Why does a baby octopus learn everything on its own? | It never meets its parents | Its parents refuse to teach it | It is born already knowing everything | It is raised by a family of crabs
exercise: Why is octopus blood blue? | It uses copper instead of iron to carry oxygen | It drinks blue sea water | It is cold all the time | It eats so many blue fish that the colour gets into its blood
exercise: How many hearts does an octopus have? | Three | One | Two | Eight
exercise: What did cuttlefish do in a test like the marshmallow test? | Waited for a liked shrimp instead of a less-liked crab | Ate everything at once | Refused all food | Hid the food under the sand for winter, like a squirrel
exercise: What do gloomy octopuses throw, and how? | Silt and shells, with a jet of water | Rocks, with their beaks | Sand, with their big eyes | Nothing at all, because they never throw
exercise: What does the mimic octopus pretend to be? | Dangerous animals like a sea snake or a flatfish | A coconut | A rock with a crab on it | A bigger octopus only, so that every fish swims away
test: «octopus has 9 brains, saw it on TikTok» What is closer to the truth? | One main brain, plus big nerve bundles in each of the eight arms | Nine brains, all the same size | No brain, just arms | One brain inside each of its three hearts, and nothing in the head
test: A shark grabs at a mimic octopus, which suddenly looks like a striped sea snake. Why would the shark back off? | Sea snakes are dangerous, so the trick scares it | The octopus turned into a real snake | Sharks hate the colour of the stripes, so they always swim off | The shark was full anyway
test: «it's a squishy blob, how's it smarter than my hamster» What is the best answer? | It solves puzzles, uses tools and escapes tanks | It is not, octopuses are not clever at all | Its whole squishy blob is one giant brain | It copies the hamster
test: What would happen if a hole in an octopus tank were bigger than its beak? | The octopus could squeeze through and escape | It would be too scared to try | It would get stuck by its bones | It would fill the hole with ink
test: A diver sees a pile of coconut shells walk across the sand. What is happening? | A veined octopus is carrying them | A whole crab family is moving house | The tide is rolling them along the sand | Fish are pushing them along the sea floor
test: In a cartoon an octopus mum walks her 8 kids to school. What is really true? | Baby octopuses never meet their parents and learn alone | Octopus mums teach their young for years | Octopus dads take the babies to school every day | Octopus babies live with their grandparents until they are grown
test: «gross, its blood is blue, is it ill» What is the answer? | No, its blood uses copper, which makes it blue | Yes, blue blood means it is very ill and needs a vet | It drank ink | It is cold and frozen
test: Count them: 2 octopuses and 1 squid swim past. How many hearts? | 9 hearts | 3 hearts | 6 hearts | 11 hearts
test: Count them: 3 octopuses. How many arms, and how many hard beaks? | 24 arms and 3 beaks | 24 arms and 24 beaks | 30 arms and 3 beaks | 27 arms and 0 beaks
test: An octopus kept squirting water at a bright lamp until it short-circuited. What does this suggest? | Octopuses can notice what bothers them and act on it | Octopuses love electricity | Octopuses want to drink from the lamps in their tanks | It was just an accident every time, nothing more than that
test: A cut-off octopus arm still tries to grab a bit of fish. Why is that possible? | Each arm has its own big bundle of nerve cells | The arm has its own brain and eyes | The arm is still a whole octopus | The fish pulls the arm
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the nervous system of the octopus? | It comprises a single central brain together with substantial nerve bundles in each of the eight arms | It comprises nine brains of equal size | It comprises no nervous tissue whatsoever | It comprises a separate brain located within each of the three hearts, the head containing none
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an octopus is able to pass through an aperture of very small dimensions. | The absence of bones, the beak being its only hard part | Its capacity to dissolve its beak at will and grow it again | The elasticity of its skeleton | Its ability to enlarge the aperture by force
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the blood of the octopus is of a blue colour. | Copper rather than iron is employed in the transport of oxygen | Sea water of a blue colour is absorbed into it through the skin | The animal is permanently chilled | Ink is mixed with the blood
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the upbringing of young octopuses. | The young never encounter their parents and must learn without instruction | The young are instructed by their mother for several years | The young are reared by their father | The young remain within the eggs for the whole of their lives
